editorialMd:
Build sectionSums

Start with the whole `readings` list as a section. Record the sum of that section. If it has more than one number, split it into a left half and a right half. Use the same steps on the left half first, then on the right half. When a section has an odd length, the left half has one fewer number. A section with one number is not split. Do not record empty sections. `sectionSums` contains the sums in the order you record them.

Return the answer

Count the even numbers in `sectionSums`. Store the count in `evenCount`. A number is even when it divides by 2 with no remainder. Complete `recursiveShelvesEvenKeyCensus` so it returns `evenCount`. Return it, do not print it.

Why this works

Each value in `sectionSums` follows task 1. The later tasks use only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(n log n); extra memory, including the helper list, O(n). n is the input length. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

javascript
function recursiveShelvesEvenKeyCensus(readings) {
  const a=readings; let out=[];
  function visit(l,r){if(l>=r)return;let s=0;for(let i=l;i<r;i++)s+=a[i];out.push(s);if(r-l>1){const m=Math.floor((l+r)/2);visit(l,m);visit(m,r);}}visit(0,a.length);
  return out.filter(x=>x%2===0).length;
}


